WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.720
 As our users, we know that sometimes code 
  is the best way to handle things. 

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.240
 And in this video walkthrough, 
  we'll be focused on setting up our access 

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.240
 to the rscloud package.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.960
 To get started using the rscloud package, 

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.440
 ee first need to set up 
  our client credentials, 

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.240
 and we do this from 
  within RStudio Cloud. 

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.640
 After logging in 
  to our RStudio Cloud account, 

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.400
 we can click on our icon or photo 
  in the top right to access the user panel 

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.760
 and then select client credentials. 

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.400
 This will take us to a page 
  with any existing credentials, 

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.360
 we might have. To create new credentials, 

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.360
 click on plus new credentials 
  in the top right. 

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.360
 This will open a pop up 
  where you can name your credentials. 

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.400
 After naming your credentials, 

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.720
 click OK to see and copy 
  your generated credentials. 

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.320
 You will only be shown 
  the credentials once. 

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.480
 So do be sure to  

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.360
 copy them and store them in a secure place 
  before closing the popup window. 

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.760
 Be careful not to share 
  or commit your credentials to git. 

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:06.560
 The recommended method for  

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.120
 setting your credentials is by editing 
  your R environ file. 

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:15.920
 And we can do this using the usethis package from within RStudio Cloud. 

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.600
 We can go to a project, 
  and by running 

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.360
 usethis::edit_r_environ() in the console. 

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.760
 This should then open your R environ file 
  where you can paste 

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.400
 and save your client credentials 
  before restarting R. 

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.560
 After setting up your credentials 
  in your R environ file, 

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.880
 you can install the development version 
  of the rscloud package by running 

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:43.200
 the following commands in the console: 
  install.packages("remotes") and 

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.600
remotes::install_github("rstudio/rscloud")  

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.360
 Once this is done, you have the rscloud  

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.000
 package available and ready to use in RStudio Cloud. 

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.560
 From here, we can do things like run 
  rscloud_space_list(), 

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.920
 which will return a table 
  with all of the spaces 

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.560
 available within our 
  our RStudio Cloud account. 

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.840
 And we can use a space_ID 

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.520
 from this table to look at the number of 
  users and projects. 

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.080
 For example, when I run rscloud_space(81721), 

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.520
 I can see that there are 327 users

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.120
 and 946 projects within this shared workspace. 

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.480
 You can learn more about additional 
  functionality of the rscloud package 

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.200
 by visiting the package 
  documentation at github.com/rstudio/rscloud

